A command-FIFO triangle packet's packed colour word is present whenever
either the RGB or the alpha parameter is enabled, not only RGB (doc 21,
M14).

Glide's packet 3 carries the per-vertex parameters named by bits 17:10 of
its header, and bit 28 says the colour comes as one packed ARGB word
rather than three floats. 86Box read that word only under the RGB bit,
and read a separate alpha float only when bit 28 was clear -- so a vertex
with iterated *alpha over a constant colour*, which Glide sends with the
packed bit set and the RGB bit clear (glide3x/cvg/glide3/src/gglide.c:
STATE_REQUIRES_IT_ALPHA without STATE_REQUIRES_IT_DRGB sets SST_SETUP_A,
sets packedRGB and adds one word per vertex), left one word per vertex in
the FIFO unread.

From there the stream is off by a word per vertex and every later packet
header is read out of the middle of somebody's parameters. What the user
saw on Windows with 3DMark 99 (2026-09-17): a loading screen drawn from
desynchronised geometry, then a hang -- with the ring through MMIO the
consumer waits inside a packet for words the guest never wrote
(`voodoo_busy` set, ring fully consumed, 15.8 M status reads in 5 s while
Glide waits for idle), and with the ring in RAM (doc 21 §9) our packet
walk, which counts words with the same rule, runs into unwritten space
and parks on a poison word while Glide polls cmdFifoRdPtr for ever
(26 M reads in 5 s, `parse` and `rp` stopped at the same address).

Both FIFO consumers in the file are fixed the same way: under bit 28 one
packed word if either parameter is named, otherwise three floats for RGB
and one for alpha, each under its own bit.

The files are 86Box's, vendored verbatim in voodoo/86box and overlaid into
hw/voodoo by prepare-qemu.sh, which is why this is a patch on the overlay
rather than an edit there. Drop it when upstream 86Box has the fix and
scripts/sync-86box-voodoo.sh brings it in.

